{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2020 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "de96e3d2210c021781ee2fa6b6dc9c9dae47826754f2cef1fdf68d73fc2c233a" :edb/db-before-t 2019 :edb/db-after-t 2020 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535332 50 #inst "2026-10-13T19:15:51.635Z" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186056460 1075 #edb/ref 17592186056461 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186056460 1075 #edb/ref 17592186056462 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186056460 1075 #edb/ref 17592186056463 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186056460 1075 #edb/ref 17592186056464 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186056460 1075 #edb/ref 17592186056465 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186056460 1075 #edb/ref 17592186496681 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186056460 1075 #edb/ref 17592186496726 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186056460 1075 #edb/ref 17592186496754 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186056460 1075 #edb/ref 17592186496853 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186056460 1075 #edb/ref 17592186496996 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186056460 1078 #edb/ref 17592186496726 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186059462 1071 "If the side that is opposite to a $45^\\circ$ angle in an isosceles right triangle has a length of $3$, what is the length of the hypotenuse?\n\n![](images/11/11036dee7004fef1c43977a8a80bb46d4277834e558ac926b851d3fd5639965b.png)" 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186059462 1071 "If the side that is opposite to a $45^\\circ$ angle in an isosceles right triangle has a length of $3,$ what is the length of the hypotenuse?\n\n![](images/11/11036dee7004fef1c43977a8a80bb46d4277834e558ac926b851d3fd5639965b.png)" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186059462 1074 "The hypotenuse in an isosceles right triangle is equal to $\\sqrt{2}$ times the length of a leg.\n\nThe legs of an isosceles right triangle are the sides that are opposite the $45^\\circ$ angles. So in the given triangle, the legs have length $3$.\n\nConsequently, the hypotenuse has a length of\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2} \\cdot 3 = 3\\sqrt{2}\n$$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186059462 1074 "The hypotenuse in an isosceles right triangle is equal to $\\sqrt{2}$ times the length of a leg.\n\nThe legs of an isosceles right triangle are the sides that are opposite the $45^\\circ$ angles. So in the given triangle, the legs have length $3.$\n\nConsequently, the hypotenuse has a length of\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2} \\cdot 3 = 3\\sqrt{2}.\n$$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186059463 1071 "If the hypotenuse of a $45^\\circ$-$45^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle has a length of $5$, then what is the length of each leg?" 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186059463 1071 "If the hypotenuse of a $45^\\circ$-$45^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle has a length of $5,$ then what is the length of each leg?" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186059463 1074 "The hypotenuse in a $45^\\circ$-$45^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle is equal to $\\sqrt{2}$ times the length of a leg.\n\nHere, we know that the hypotenuse has length $5$. So, if the length of a leg is $a$, then we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5 &= \\sqrt{2} \\cdot a \\\\\na &= \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the square root from the denominator, we can multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{2}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na &= \\frac{5 \\cdot \\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{2}} \\\\\na &= \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of each leg is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n$$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186059463 1074 "The hypotenuse in a $45^\\circ$-$45^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle is equal to $\\sqrt{2}$ times the length of a leg.\n\nHere, we know that the hypotenuse has length $5.$ So, if the length of a leg is $a,$ then we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot a \\\\ a & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the square root from the denominator, we can multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{5\\cdot \\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}} \\\\ a & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of each leg is $\\dfrac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186059464 1071 "Find the length of the diagonal $\\overline{AC}$ of the square $ABCD$ if the perimeter of the square is $p=256\\,\\mathrm{mm}$." 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186059464 1071 "Find the length of the diagonal $\\overline{AC}$ of the square $ABCD$ if the perimeter of the square is $p=256\\,\\mathrm{mm}.$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186059464 1074 "Since the perimeter is\n\n$$\np=256\\,\\textrm{mm}\n$$\n\nthe length of a single side is\n\n$$\nAB = \\dfrac{p}{4} = \\dfrac{256}{4} = 64\\,\\mathrm{mm}\n$$\n\nNow, consider $\\triangle{ABC}$, which is an isosceles right triangle since\n\n$$\n\\angle{ABC} = 90^\\circ\n$$\n\nand $AB=BC$.\n\n![](images/5b/5bb28a161b88919351ee8e83c2943817f20c124d80fb24c4ceffe29129fbbb07.png)\n\nThe hypotenuse in an isosceles right triangle is equal to $\\sqrt{2}$ times the length of a leg.\n\nHere, the length of a leg is $AB=64$, so the length of the hypotenuse is\n\n$$\nAC = \\sqrt{2}\\cdot AB = \\sqrt{2}\\cdot 64 = 64\\sqrt{2}\\,\\textrm{mm}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the diagonal of the square $ABCD$ has a length of\n\n$$\nAC= 64 \\sqrt{2} \\, \\textrm{mm}\n$$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186059464 1074 "Since the perimeter is $p=256\\,\\textrm{mm},$ the length of a single side is\n\n$$\nAB = \\dfrac{p}{4} = \\dfrac{256}{4} = 64\\,\\mathrm{mm}.\n$$\n\nNow, consider $\\triangle{ABC}$, which is an isosceles right triangle since $\\angle{ABC} = 90^\\circ$ and $AB=BC.$\n\n![](images/5b/5bb28a161b88919351ee8e83c2943817f20c124d80fb24c4ceffe29129fbbb07.png)\n\nThe hypotenuse in an isosceles right triangle is equal to $\\sqrt{2}$ times the length of a leg.\n\nHere, the length of a leg is $AB=64,$ so the length of the hypotenuse is\n\n$$\nAC = \\sqrt{2}\\cdot AB = \\sqrt{2}\\cdot 64 = 64\\sqrt{2}\\,\\textrm{mm}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the diagonal of the square $ABCD$ has a length of $AC= 64 \\sqrt{2} \\, \\textrm{mm}.$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186059466 1071 "The perimeter of the triangle below is $26+13\\sqrt{2}$. Find the measure of the hypotenuse.\n\n![](images/d1/d194f2c2ce34bd40f5da5dd9e30048a6cc9318a1308c8a7e5d7156cb58a76134.png)" 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186059466 1071 "The perimeter of the triangle below is $26+13\\sqrt{2}.$ Find the measure of the hypotenuse.\n\n![](images/d1/d194f2c2ce34bd40f5da5dd9e30048a6cc9318a1308c8a7e5d7156cb58a76134.png)" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186059466 1074 "Because the triangle is isosceles, the legs must have the same length. Let $a$ be the length of each leg. So, we have\n\n$$\nAB=BC=a\n$$\n\nMoreover, because the triangle is a *right* isosceles triangle, the hypotenuse must be equal to $\\sqrt{2}$ times the length of a leg. Therefore,\n\n$$\nAC = AB\\cdot\\sqrt{2} = a\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the perimeter is\n\n$$\np = 26+13\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np &= AB + BC + AC \\\\\n26 + 13\\sqrt{2} &= a + a + a\\sqrt{2} \\\\\n26 + 13\\sqrt{2} &= 2a + a\\sqrt{2} \\\\\n13(2 + \\sqrt{2}) &= a(2 + \\sqrt{2}) \\\\\n13(2 + \\sqrt{2}) &= a(2 + \\sqrt{2}) \\\\\n13 &= a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of each leg is $a=13$, and the length of the hypotenuse is\n\n$$\nAC = a \\sqrt{2} = 13 \\sqrt{2}\n$$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186059466 1074 "Because the triangle is isosceles, the legs must have the same length. Let $a$ be the length of each leg. So, we have\n\n$$\nAB=BC=a.\n$$\n\nMoreover, because the triangle is a *right* isosceles triangle, the hypotenuse must be equal to $\\sqrt{2}$ times the length of a leg. Therefore,\n\n$$\nAC = AB\\cdot\\sqrt{2} = a\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since the perimeter is $p = 26+13\\sqrt{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =AB+BC+AC \\\\ 26+13\\sqrt{2} & =a+a+a\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 26+13\\sqrt{2} & =2a+a\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 13(2+\\sqrt{2}) & =a(2+\\sqrt{2}) \\\\ 13\\cancel{(2+\\sqrt{2})} & =a\\cancel{(2+\\sqrt{2})} \\\\ 13 & =a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of each leg is $a=13,$ and the length of the hypotenuse is\n\n$$\nAC = a \\sqrt{2} = 13 \\sqrt{2} .\n$$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186059615 1071 "If $X = \\{ a, b, c, d, e, f, g \\},$ which of the following collections of subsets of $X$ are partitions of $X?$\n\n- $\\{ a, b, \\{ c,d,e \\}, \\{ f,g \\} \\}$\n\n- $\\{ \\{ a, b, c \\}, \\{ d,e,f \\}, \\{ g \\} \\}$\n\n- $\\{ \\emptyset, \\{ a,b,c \\}, \\{ f,g \\} \\}$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186059615 1071 "If $X = \\{ a, b, c, d, e, f, g \\},$ which of the following collections of subsets of $X$ are partitions of $X?$\n\nI. $\\{ a, b, \\{ c,d,e \\}, \\{ f,g \\} \\}$\n\nII. $\\{ \\{ a, b, c \\}, \\{ d,e,f \\}, \\{ g \\} \\}$\n\nIII. $\\{ \\emptyset, \\{ a,b,c \\}, \\{ f,g \\} \\}$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186059617 1071 "If $X = \\{ 5, 10, 15, \\ldots, 50 \\},$ which of the following collections of subsets of $X$ are partitions of $X?$\n\n- $\\{ \\{ 5, 10 \\}, \\{ 15, 20, 25, \\ldots, 45 \\}, \\{ 50 \\} \\}$\n\n- $\\{ \\{ 5, 10, 15, \\ldots, 30 \\}, \\{ 40, 45, 50 \\} \\}$\n\n- $\\{ \\{ 5, 10, 15, \\ldots, 50 \\} \\}$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186059617 1071 "If $X = \\{ 5, 10, 15, \\ldots, 50 \\},$ which of the following collections of subsets of $X$ are partitions of $X?$\n\nI. $\\{ \\{ 5, 10 \\}, \\{ 15, 20, 25, \\ldots, 45 \\}, \\{ 50 \\} \\}$\n\nII. $\\{ \\{ 5, 10, 15, \\ldots, 30 \\}, \\{ 40, 45, 50 \\} \\}$\n\nIII. $\\{ \\{ 5, 10, 15, \\ldots, 50 \\} \\}$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186059630 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497361 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186059630 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497375 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186059631 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497351 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186059631 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497385 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186059636 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497395 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186059636 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497406 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186059638 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497389 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186059638 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497415 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186059812 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497365 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186059812 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497379 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186059813 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497326 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186059814 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497404 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186059918 1050 420.0 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186059938 1050 600.0 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186060017 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186060017 1071 "If $X = {t, u, v, w, x, y, z}$, which collection of subsets of $X$ is a partition of $X$?" 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186060017 1071 "If $X=\\{t,u,v,w,x,y,z\\},$ which collection of subsets of $X$ is a partition of $X?$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186060017 1074 "A partition of a set $X$ is a collection of non-empty subsets of $X$ where every element of $X$ is contained in *exactly* one subset. \n\nOf the given collections, only $\\{\\{t,u,v\\},\\{w\\},\\{x,y,z\\}\\}$ is a partition.\n\nThe rest of the collections are not partitions:\n\n- $\\{∅,\\{t,u,v,w\\},\\{x,y,z\\}\\}$ is not a partition because the subset $∅$ is empty.\n\n- $\\{∅,\\{t\\},\\{u,v,w\\},\\{x,y,z\\}\\}$ is not a partition because the subset $∅$ is empty.\n\n- $\\{\\{t,u,v\\},\\{v,w\\},\\{v,w,x,y,z\\}\\}$ is not a partition because the element $v$ appears in three subsets.\n\n- $\\{\\{t,u,v\\},\\{y,z\\}\\}$ is not a partition because the elements $w$ and $x$ do not appear in any subsets." 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186060405 1068 #edb/ref 17592186059052 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186060405 1071 "Consider the set $X=\\{3,6,9,12,15,18,21,24\\}.$ Which subset must be placed into the blank in the set below so that it represents a partition of $X?$\n\n$$\n\\{\\{6,12,18\\},\\{15,21\\},\\,____\\,\\}\n$$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186060405 1071 "Consider the set $X={3,6,9,12,15,18,21,24}.$ Which subset must be placed into the blank in the set below so that it represents a partition of $X?$\n\n$$\n{{6,12,18},{15,21},\\,____\\,}\n$$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186060405 1074 "A partition of a set $X$ is a collection of non-empty subsets of $X$ where every element of $X$ is contained in *exactly* one subset. \n\nTo find the subset that goes in the blank, we must identify which elements of $X$ are not yet contained within any of the given subsets.\n\nLet's go through each element of $X$ and check:\n\n- $3$ does not belong to any of the given subsets $\\,\\times $\n\n- $6\\in \\{6,12,18\\}\\,✓$\n\n- $9$ does not belong to any of the given subsets $\\,\\times $\n\n- $12\\in \\{6,12,18\\}\\,✓$\n\n- $15\\in \\{15,21\\}\\,✓$\n\n- $18\\in \\{6,12,18\\}\\,✓$\n\n- $21\\in \\{15,21\\}\\,✓$\n\n- $24$ does not belong to any of the given subsets $\\,\\times $\n\nThe elements $3,$ $9,$ and $24$ are not yet contained within any of the given subsets. Therefore, the subset that must go in the blank is $\\{3,9,24\\}.$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186060405 1074 "A partition of a set $X$ is a collection of non-empty subsets of $X$ where every element of $X$ is contained in *exactly* one subset. \n\nTo find the subset that goes in the blank, we must identify which elements of $X$ are not yet contained within any of the given subsets.\n\nLet's go through each element of $X$ and check:\n\n- $3$ does not belong to any of the given subsets $\\,\\times $\n\n- $6\\in {6,12,18}\\,✓$\n\n- $9$ does not belong to any of the given subsets $\\,\\times $\n\n- $12\\in {6,12,18}\\,✓$\n\n- $15\\in {15,21}\\,✓$\n\n- $18\\in {6,12,18}\\,✓$\n\n- $21\\in {15,21}\\,✓$\n\n- $24$ does not belong to any of the given subsets $\\,\\times $\n\nThe elements $3,$ $9,$ and $24$ are not yet contained within any of the given subsets. Therefore, the subset that must go in the blank is ${3,9,24}.$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186060872 1064 "In isosceles right triangles such as the one shown below, the angles always measure $45^\\circ$, $45^\\circ$, and $90^\\circ$.\n\nConsequently, isosceles right triangles are often called **$\\boldsymbol{45^\\circ}$-$\\boldsymbol{45^\\circ}$-$\\boldsymbol{90^\\circ}$ triangles**.\n\n![](images/d3/d32159528abc75f3ed8bcccaedb2765b676fd6cc64164e2f8c81f98eb37f6db4.png)\n\nAll $45^\\circ$-$45^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangles share a special property:\n\n> *In any ${45^\\circ}$-${45^\\circ}$-${90^\\circ}$ triangle, the length of the hypotenuse is $\\sqrt{2}$ times the length of a leg.*\n\nTo see why this is, remember that\n\n- in an isosceles triangle, the two legs must have the same length, and\n- in a right triangle, the square of the longest side must equal the sum of the squares of the two shorter sides.\n\nSo, if an isosceles right triangle has legs of length $a$ and the hypotenuse has length $h$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nh^{2} &= a^{2} + a^{2} \\\\\nh^{2} &= 2a^{2} \\\\\nh &= \\sqrt{2a^{2}} \\\\\nh &= \\sqrt{2} \\cdot a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186060872 1064 "In isosceles right triangles such as the one shown below, the angles always measure $45^\\circ,$ $45^\\circ,$ and $90^\\circ.$ \n\nConsequently, isosceles right triangles are often called **$\\boldsymbol{45^\\circ}$-$\\boldsymbol{45^\\circ}$-$\\boldsymbol{90^\\circ}$ triangles**.\n\n![](images/d3/d32159528abc75f3ed8bcccaedb2765b676fd6cc64164e2f8c81f98eb37f6db4.png)\n\nAll $45^\\circ$-$45^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangles share a special property:\n\n*In any ${45^\\circ}$-${45^\\circ}$-${90^\\circ}$ triangle, the length of the hypotenuse is $\\sqrt{2}$ times the length of a leg.*\n\nTo see why this is, remember that\n\n- in an isosceles triangle, the two legs must have the same length, and\n\n- in a right triangle, the square of the longest side must equal the sum of the squares of the two shorter sides.\n\nSo, if an isosceles right triangle has legs of length $a$ and the hypotenuse has length $h,$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{2} & ={a}^{2}+{a}^{2} \\\\ {h}^{2} & =2{a}^{2} \\\\ h & =\\sqrt{2{a}^{2}} \\\\ h & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot a.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186060873 1064 "Now that we know about $45^\\circ$-$45^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangles, it's straightforward to see that the diagonal of any square is $\\sqrt{2}$ times the side length of the square.\n\nTo illustrate, consider the square below, where $a$ is the measure of a side and $c$ is the measure of a diagonal.\n\n![](images/07/0743e533eefd4c7e51eaca51c9285fc1b6277553d4f214602a65fc50d1d69ace.png)\n\nSince the sides of a square are congruent, $\\triangle{ABC}$ is an isosceles right triangle (a $45^{\\circ}$-$45^{\\circ}$-$90^{\\circ}$ triangle).\n\nSo, for the diagonal, we have\n\n$$\nc = \\sqrt{2} \\cdot a\n$$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186060873 1064 "Now that we know about $45^\\circ$-$45^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangles, it's straightforward to see that the diagonal of any square is $\\sqrt{2}$ times the side length of the square.\n\nTo illustrate, consider the square below, where $a$ is the measure of a side and $c$ is the measure of a diagonal.\n\n![](images/07/0743e533eefd4c7e51eaca51c9285fc1b6277553d4f214602a65fc50d1d69ace.png)\n\nSince the sides of a square are congruent, $\\triangle{ABC}$ is an isosceles right triangle (a $45^{\\circ}$-$45^{\\circ}$-$90^{\\circ}$ triangle). \n\nSo, for the diagonal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c=\\sqrt{2}\\cdot a.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186060958 1064 "A **partition** of a set $A$ is a collection of non-empty subsets of $A$ where every element of $A$ is contained in *exactly* one subset.\n\nIn other words, none of the subsets can be the null set $(\\emptyset),$ and every element of $A$ must be contained in one *and only one* subset.\n\nFor example, the following collections of subsets of $X=\\{1, 2, 3, 4, 5\\}$ are all partitions:\n\n- $X_1 = \\{ \\{ 1, 2\\}, \\{ 3, 4 \\}, \\{5\\} \\}$\n- $X_2 = \\{ \\{ 1, 2, 3\\}, \\{4, 5\\} \\}$\n- $X_3 = \\{ \\{ 1, 3\\}, \\{ 2\\}, \\{4, 5\\} \\}$\n- $X_4 = \\{ \\{ 1, 4, 5\\}, \\{ 2, 3\\} \\}$\n- $X_5 = \\{ \\{ 1, 2, 3, 4\\}, \\{5\\} \\}$\n\nHowever, the following collections are *not* partitions:\n\n- $X_6 = \\{ \\{ 1\\}, \\{ 3, 4 \\}, \\{5\\} \\}$ - the number $2$ is not included in any subset\n- $X_7 = \\{ \\emptyset, \\{ 1, 2, 3\\}, \\{4, 5\\} \\}$ - includes $\\emptyset$\n- $X_8 = \\{ \\{ 1, 3\\}, \\{ 2, 3\\}, \\{4, 5\\} \\}$ - the number $3$ is included in two different subsets\n- $X_9 = \\{ \\{ 1, 2 \\}, \\{ 2, 3\\} \\}$ - missing $4$ and $5,$ and $2$ is included in two different subsets\n- $X_{10} = \\{ 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 \\}$ - this not a collection of subsets" 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186060958 1064 "A **partition** of a set $A$ is a collection of non-empty subsets of $A$ where every element of $A$ is contained in *exactly* one subset. \n\nIn other words, none of the subsets can be the null set $(\\emptyset),$ and every element of $A$ must be contained in one *and only one* subset.\n\nFor example, the following collections of subsets of $X=\\{1, 2, 3, 4, 5\\}$ are all partitions:\n\n- $X_1 = \\{ \\{ 1, 2\\}, \\{ 3, 4 \\}, \\{5\\} \\}$\n\n- $X_2 = \\{ \\{ 1, 2, 3\\}, \\{4, 5\\} \\}$\n\n- $X_3 = \\{ \\{ 1, 3\\}, \\{ 2\\}, \\{4, 5\\} \\}$\n\n- $X_4 = \\{ \\{ 1, 4, 5\\}, \\{ 2, 3\\} \\}$\n\n- $X_5 = \\{ \\{ 1, 2, 3, 4\\}, \\{5\\} \\}$\n\nHowever, the following collections are *not* partitions:\n\n- $X_6 = \\{ \\{ 1\\}, \\{ 3, 4 \\}, \\{5\\} \\}$ - the number $2$ is not included in any subset\n\n- $X_7 = \\{ \\emptyset, \\{ 1, 2, 3\\}, \\{4, 5\\} \\}$ - includes $\\emptyset$\n\n- $X_8 = \\{ \\{ 1, 3\\}, \\{ 2, 3\\}, \\{4, 5\\} \\}$ - the number $3$ is included in two different subsets\n\n- $X_9 = \\{ \\{ 1, 2 \\}, \\{ 2, 3\\} \\}$ - missing $4$ and $5,$ and $2$ is included in two different subsets\n\n- $X_{10} = \\{ 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 \\}$ - this not a collection of subsets" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186067750 1075 #edb/ref 17592186067751 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186067750 1075 #edb/ref 17592186067752 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186067750 1075 #edb/ref 17592186067753 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186067750 1075 #edb/ref 17592186067754 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186067750 1075 #edb/ref 17592186067755 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186067750 1075 #edb/ref 17592186496969 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186067750 1075 #edb/ref 17592186496972 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186067750 1075 #edb/ref 17592186497095 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186067750 1075 #edb/ref 17592186497147 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186067750 1075 #edb/ref 17592186497182 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186067750 1078 #edb/ref 17592186496969 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186068542 1071 "What is the circumference $C$ of a circle with a diameter of $d=10$?\n\n![](images/b1/b1ebd81fca9688b5688dc916d6be62bad14853f55a8e66c5c7af469dd4032ec4.png)" 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186068542 1071 "What is the circumference $C$ of a circle with a diameter of $d=10?$ \n\n![](images/b1/b1ebd81fca9688b5688dc916d6be62bad14853f55a8e66c5c7af469dd4032ec4.png)" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186068542 1074 "We use the formula $C=\\pi d$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC &= πd \\\\\n&= π \\cdot 10 \\\\\n&= 10π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the exact answer. If we want an approximate answer, say rounded to three decimal places, we can use the approximation\n\n$$\n\\pi\\approx 3.14\\,159\n$$\n\nand we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC &= 10 \\cdot π \\\\\n&\\approx 10 \\cdot 3.14159 \\\\\n&\\approx 31.416\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186068542 1074 "We use the formula $C=\\pi d$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\pi d \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot 10 \\\\  & =10\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the exact answer. If we want an approximate answer, say rounded to three decimal places, we can use the approximation $\\pi\\approx 3.14\\,159,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =10\\cdot \\pi  \\\\  & ≈10\\cdot 3.14\\,159 \\\\  & ≈31.416.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186068543 1071 "What is the circumference $C$ of a circle with a radius of $6$?\n\n![](images/3c/3c21021529285f488ca794a442e5826a033ed2a4c6f4fad53ab9e8fdc84d35ee.png)" 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186068543 1071 "What is the circumference $C$ of a circle with a radius of $6?$\n\n![](images/3c/3c21021529285f488ca794a442e5826a033ed2a4c6f4fad53ab9e8fdc84d35ee.png)" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186068543 1074 "We use the formula $C=2\\pi r$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC &= 2πr \\\\\n&= 2π \\cdot 6 \\\\\n&= 12π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186068543 1074 "We use the formula $C=2\\pi r$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2\\pi r \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot 6 \\\\  & =12\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186068544 1071 "What is the radius $r$ of a circle with a circumference of $C=17\\pi$?\n\n![](images/69/697cc9ca06bcfe46d84dfab74e0f6ae83f4342607ea4af138b111606c16e8a03.png)" 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186068544 1071 "What is the radius $r$ of a circle with a circumference of $C=17\\pi?$\n\n![](images/69/697cc9ca06bcfe46d84dfab74e0f6ae83f4342607ea4af138b111606c16e8a03.png)" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186068544 1074 "We use the formula for the circumference $C=2\\pi r$ and solve for $r$. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC &= 2πr \\\\\n17π &= 2πr \\\\\nr &= \\frac{17π}{2π} \\\\\n&= \\frac{17}{2} \\\\\n&= 8.5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186068544 1074 "We use the formula for the circumference $C=2\\pi r$ and solve for $r.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2\\pi r \\\\ 17\\pi  & =2\\pi r \\\\ r & =\\frac{17\\pi }{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{2} \\\\  & =8.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186068545 1071 "Carol has to wrap a rope around a circular column whose diameter is $20\\,\\textrm{cm}$. What length of the rope will wrap the column perfectly?" 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186068545 1071 "Carol has to wrap a rope around a circular column whose diameter is $20\\,\\textrm{cm}.$ What length of the rope will wrap the column perfectly?" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186068545 1074 "The length of the rope needed to wrap around the column is its circumference.\n\nThe formula for the circumference of a circle is $C = \\pi d$, where\n\n$$\nd=20\\,\\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\nis the diameter.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nC = \\pi\\cdot 20 = 20\\pi \\,\\textrm{cm}\n$$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186068545 1074 "The length of the rope needed to wrap around the column is its circumference.\n\nThe formula for the circumference of a circle is $C = \\pi d$, where $d=20\\,\\textrm{cm}$ is the diameter. \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nC = \\pi\\cdot 20 = 20\\pi \\,\\textrm{cm}.\n$$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186068681 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497391 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186068681 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497396 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186068682 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497332 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186068682 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497335 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186068683 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497338 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186068683 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497366 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186068683 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497399 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186068684 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497327 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186068684 1039 "Calculating the Circumference of a Circle: Word Problem" 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186068684 1039 "Word Problems" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186068987 1043 #edb/ref 17592186497477 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186068987 1050 480.0 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186069430 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186069430 1071 "![](images/ad/ad42b4bd62bf64e1ac086a60ace286b3e6d1c136f423d2ae6aabf6339cea9071.png)\n\nWhat length of fence is needed to border a circular plot of land with a diameter of $30\\,\\text{m}?$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186069430 1071 "![](images/ad/ad42b4bd62bf64e1ac086a60ace286b3e6d1c136f423d2ae6aabf6339cea9071.png)\n\nWhat length of fence is needed to border a circular plot of land with a diameter of $30m$?" 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186069430 1074 "The length of the fence needed to border the circular land is its circumference.\n\nThe formula for the circumference of a circle is $C=\\pi d,$ where $d=30\\,\\text{m}$ is the diameter.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nC=\\pi \\cdot 30=30\\pi \\,\\text{m}.\n$$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186069550 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069551 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186069550 1058 #edb/ref 17592186497477 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186069956 1064 "The **circumference** of a circle is the length of the path that goes around the circle.\n\n![](images/ac/ac8f31a99961c51cb53695d9daab017126d1316b720e6383b876824d7c7947bd.png)\n\nThe circumference is usually denoted by the letter $C$.\n\nOne important fact about the circumference is that, for *any* circle, no matter how small or large it is, the ratio of circumference $C$ to diameter $d$ is a fixed number! Specifically,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{C}{d} = {\\color{blue}\\boldsymbol{\\pi}} \\approx 3.141\\,592\\,654..\n$$\n\nThis important irrational number is called **pi** (pronounced \"pie\") and we use the Greek letter $\\pi$ to express it. So, we can calculate the circumference by using the following formula:\n\n$$\nC = πd\n$$\n\nHere, our circle has diameter $4\\,\\textrm{cm}$, so its circumference is\n\n$$\nC = \\pi \\cdot 4 = 4\\pi \\approx 12.566\\,\\mathrm{cm}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal digits.\n\nSince the diameter is twice as long as the radius ($d=2r$), we can also write the formula for the circumference as\n\n$$\nC = \\pi d = 2\\pi r\n$$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186069956 1064 "The **circumference** of a circle is the length of the path that goes around the circle.\n\n![](images/ac/ac8f31a99961c51cb53695d9daab017126d1316b720e6383b876824d7c7947bd.png)\n\nThe circumference is usually denoted by the letter $C.$\n\nOne important fact about the circumference is that, for *any* circle, no matter how small or large it is, the ratio of circumference $C$ to diameter $d$ is a fixed number! Specifically,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{C}{d} = {\\color{blue}\\boldsymbol{\\pi}} \\approx 3.141\\,592\\,654...\n$$\n\n \n\nThis important irrational number is called **pi** (pronounced \"pie\") and we use the Greek letter $\\pi$ to express it. So, we can calculate the circumference by using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C=\\pi d.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, our circle has diameter $4\\,\\textrm{cm},$ so its circumference is\n\n$$\nC = \\pi \\cdot 4 = 4\\pi \\approx 12.566\\,\\mathrm{cm},\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal digits.\n\nSince the diameter is twice as long as the radius ($d=2r$), we can also write the formula for the circumference as\n\n$$\nC = \\pi d = 2\\pi r.\n$$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186073752 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497411 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186081935 1075 #edb/ref 17592186081936 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186081935 1075 #edb/ref 17592186081937 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186081935 1075 #edb/ref 17592186081938 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186081935 1075 #edb/ref 17592186081939 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186081935 1075 #edb/ref 17592186081940 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186081935 1075 #edb/ref 17592186496670 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186081935 1075 #edb/ref 17592186496867 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186081935 1075 #edb/ref 17592186496876 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186081935 1075 #edb/ref 17592186496953 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186081935 1075 #edb/ref 17592186497091 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186081935 1078 #edb/ref 17592186496867 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186082921 1071 "Is it possible that the lengths of the sides of a triangle are $10, 15$, and $25$?" 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186082921 1071 "Is it possible that the lengths of the sides of a triangle are $10, 15,$ and $25?$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186082921 1074 "The triangle inequality theorem states that the length of any one side must be smaller than the sum of the other two sides.\n\nIn particular, for the numbers $10,15,25$ to represent the sides of a triangle, the number $25$ must be smaller than the sum of the numbers $10$ and $15$. But this is not the case:\n\n$$\n10 + 15 = 25 \\not> 25\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three numbers $10,15,25$ cannot represent the sides of a triangle." 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186082921 1074 "The triangle inequality theorem states that the length of any one side must be smaller than the sum of the other two sides.\n\nIn particular, for the numbers $10,15,25$ to represent the sides of a triangle, the number $25$ must be smaller than the sum of the numbers $10$ and $15.$ But this is not the case:\n\n$$\n10 + 15 = 25 \\not> 25\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three numbers $10,15,25$ cannot represent the sides of a triangle." 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186082922 1071 "A triangle has sides of length $1,7$, and $x$. Use the triangle inequality to determine all possible values of $x$." 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186082922 1071 "A triangle has sides of length $1,7,$ and $x.$ Use the triangle inequality to determine all possible values of $x.$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186082922 1074 "According to the triangle inequality theorem, the length of any side must be\n\n- greater than the difference between the lengths of the other two sides, and\n\n- less than the sum of those lengths.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Difference} & <x<\\text{Sum} \\\\ 7-1 & <x<7+1 \\\\ 6 & <x<8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186082922 1074 "According to the triangle inequality theorem, the length of any side must be\n\n- greater than the difference between the lengths of the other two sides, and\n- less than the sum of those lengths.\n\nTherefore, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}Difference & < x < Sum \\\\ 7 - 1 & < x < 7 + 1 \\\\ 6 & < x < 8\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186082923 1071 "Paul knows that the distance between cell tower $A$ and cell tower $B$ is $35\\,\\textrm{miles}$, and the distance between cell tower $B$ and cell tower $C$ is $25\\,\\textrm{miles}$, but he does not know their exact locations. Assuming that the three locations form a triangle, what is the range of possible values for the distance between cellphone towers $A$ and $C$?" 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186082923 1071 "Paul knows that the distance between cell tower $A$ and cell tower $B$ is $35\\,\\textrm{miles},$ and the distance between cell tower $B$ and cell tower $C$ is $25\\,\\textrm{miles},$ but he does not know their exact locations. Assuming that the three locations form a triangle, what is the range of possible values for the distance between cellphone towers $A$ and $C?$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186082923 1074 "First, we let:\n\n- $A$ be the position of the tower $A$\n- $B$ be the position of the tower $B$\n- $C$ be the position of the tower $C$\n\nLet's draw $\\triangle ABC$.\n\n![](images/a7/a74e768b739cf6ea497af934a44f0a5616da191b7f1a1c0979673a7ca891b0d6.png)\n\nWe know that\n\n$$\nAB = 35\\,\\textrm{mi}, \\qquad BC = 25\\,\\textrm{mi}\n$$\n\nAccording to the triangle inequality theorem,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}AB - BC & < AC < AB + BC \\\\ 35 - 25 & < AC < 35 + 25 \\\\ 10 & < AC < 60\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nTherefore, the distance from the tower $A$ to the tower $C$ must be *between $10 \\,\\textrm{mi}$ and $60 \\,\\textrm{mi}$*." 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186082923 1074 "First, we let: \n\n- $A$ be the position of the tower $A$\n\n- $B$ be the position of the tower $B$\n\n- $C$ be the position of the tower $C$\n\nLet's draw $\\triangle ABC.$\n\n![](images/a7/a74e768b739cf6ea497af934a44f0a5616da191b7f1a1c0979673a7ca891b0d6.png)\n\nWe know that\n\n$$\nAB = 35\\,\\textrm{mi},  \\qquad  BC = 25\\,\\textrm{mi}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the triangle inequality theorem,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB-BC & <AC<AB+BC \\\\ 35-25 & <AC<35+25 \\\\ 10 & <AC<60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance from the tower $A$ to the tower $C$ must be *between $10 \\,\\textrm{mi}$ and $60 \\,\\textrm{mi}$*." 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186082924 1071 "![](images/63/6301ab046f34d3261395d10ba73d31f62262c2362d0b088b38f67354568af97e.png)\n\nUsing the triangle inequality theorem only, what is the best upper bound that can be obtained for the length of $\\overline{AC}$?\n(*Note: Figure not drawn to scale*)" 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186082924 1071 "![](images/63/6301ab046f34d3261395d10ba73d31f62262c2362d0b088b38f67354568af97e.png)\n\nUsing the triangle inequality theorem only, what is the best upper bound that can be obtained for the length of $\\overline{AC}?$\n(*Note: Figure not drawn to scale*)" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186082924 1074 "The triangle inequality theorem states that the length of any one side must be smaller than the sum of the other two sides.\n\n- Applying the triangle inequality theorem to $\\triangle{ADC}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & <AD+DC \\\\ AC & <26\\,\\text{cm}+13\\,\\text{cm} \\\\ AC & <39\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Applying the triangle inequality theorem to $\\triangle{ABC}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & <AB+BC \\\\ AC & <12\\,\\text{cm}+10\\,\\text{cm} \\\\ AC & <22\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both $AC <          39\\,\\textrm{cm}$ and $AC <          22\\,\\textrm{cm}$ must be satisfied, we conclude that \n\n$$\nAC <          22\\,\\textrm{cm}.\n$$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186082924 1074 "The triangle inequality theorem states that the length of any one side must be smaller than the sum of the other two sides.\n\n- Applying the triangle inequality theorem to $\\triangle{ADC}$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}AC & < AD + DC \\\\ AC & < 26cm + 13cm \\\\ AC & < 39cm\\end{bmatrix}$.\n- Applying the triangle inequality theorem to $\\triangle{ABC}$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}AC & < AB + BC \\\\ AC & < 12cm + 10cm \\\\ AC & < 22cm\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nSince both $AC < 39\\,\\textrm{cm}$ and $AC < 22\\,\\textrm{cm}$ must be satisfied, we conclude that\n\n$$\nAC < 22\\,\\textrm{cm}\n$$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186083395 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497387 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186083396 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497378 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186083396 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497408 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186083397 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497337 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186083397 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497397 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186083398 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497347 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186083398 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497376 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186083455 1050 600.0 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186083834 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186083834 1074 "The triangle inequality theorem states that the length of any one side must be smaller than the sum of the other two sides.\n\nIn particular, for the numbers $17,9,8$ to represent the sides of a triangle, the number $17$ must be smaller than the sum of the numbers $9$ and $8.$ But this is not the case:\n\n$$\n9+8=17≯17\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three numbers $17,9,8$ cannot represent the sides of a triangle." 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186084509 1064 "Let $\\triangle{ABC}$ be a triangle where the lengths $AB=7$ and $AC=5$ are fixed. For example, consider the triangle shown below.\n\n![](images/e4/e4c1df256d9a40c50e3535f3055fef041cf02710c0a9e0414acae1bf38681ff4.png)\n\n      \n\nNow, suppose that we vary the angle $\\angle{A}$, making it either very small or very large. What would be the maximum possible length of the side $\\overline {BC}$ for which the figure is still a triangle?  \n\nWe make the longest possible segment $\\overline{BC}$ by increasing the angle $\\angle{A}$ as shown in the sketches below. Here, we fix vertices $A$ and $B$, and then \"rotate\" the side $\\overline{AC}$ about the vertex $A$ until $\\angle{A}$ measures $180^\\circ.$\n\n![](images/d9/d9777d021ad9aac118452b691567d325939c5c8591b777f2cf9c17b39916e660.png)\n\n          \n\nFrom the picture, we can see that when $m\\angle{A}=180^\\circ ,$ we will not have a triangle anymore and the length of $\\overline{BC}$ will be $7+5=12.$ Therefore, if we would like to have a triangle, $\\overline{BC}$ must be shorter than the sum of the other sides: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & <AB+AC \\\\  & <7+5 \\\\  & <12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is true for all triangles:\n\nIn every triangle, the length of any one side must be smaller than the sum of the other two sides.\n\nThe above statement is one part of the **triangle inequality theorem**, which we will continue building upon in this lesson." 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186084509 1064 "Let $\\triangle{ABC}$ be a triangle where the lengths $AB=7$ and $AC=5$ are fixed. For example, consider the triangle shown below.\n\n![](images/e4/e4c1df256d9a40c50e3535f3055fef041cf02710c0a9e0414acae1bf38681ff4.png)\n\nNow, suppose that we vary the angle $\\angle{A}$, making it either very small or very large. What would be the maximum possible length of the side $\\overline {BC}$ for which the figure is still a triangle?\n\nWe make the longest possible segment $\\overline{BC}$ by increasing the angle $\\angle{A}$ as shown in the sketches below. Here, we fix vertices $A$ and $B$, and then \"rotate\" the side $\\overline{AC}$ about the vertex $A$ until $\\angle{A}$ measures $180^\\circ$.\n\n![](images/d9/d9777d021ad9aac118452b691567d325939c5c8591b777f2cf9c17b39916e660.png)\n\nFrom the picture, we can see that when\n\n$$\nm\\angle{A}=180^\\circ\n$$\n\nwe will not have a triangle anymore and the length of $\\overline{BC}$ will be\n\n$$\n7+5=12\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we would like to have a triangle, $\\overline{BC}$ must be shorter than the sum of the other sides:\n$BC \\mid < AB + AC; < 7 + 5; < 12$\n\nThis is true for all triangles:\n\n> In every triangle, the length of any one side must be smaller than the sum of the other two sides.\n\nThe above statement is one part of the **triangle inequality theorem**, which we will continue building upon in this lesson." 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186084510 1064 "Consider the same triangle as before, with sides $5$ and $7$. This time, what is the shortest possible length of the side $\\overline {BC}$, for which the figure is still a triangle?\n\nWe make the shortest possible segment $\\overline{BC}$ by decreasing the angle $\\angle{A}$. Again, we fix $A$ and $B$, and then \"rotate\" the side $\\overline{AC}$ about the vertex $A$ towards the side $\\overline{AB}$.\n\n![](images/7d/7d1bd3363c2c218c6a2813a957bace3d29b7240919ced63d58be299e9478c099.png)\n\nFrom the picture, we can see that for\n\n$$\nm\\angle{A}=0^\\circ\n$$\n\nwe will not have a triangle anymore and the length of $\\overline{BC}$ will be $7-5=2$. Therefore, if we would like to have a triangle, $\\overline{BC}$ must be longer than the difference of the other sides:\n$BC \\mid > AB - AC; > 7 - 5; > 2$\n\nThis is true for all triangles:\n\n> In every triangle, the length of any one side must be larger than the difference of the other two sides.\n\nThe above statement is the other part of the **triangle inequality theorem**." 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186084510 1064 "Consider the same triangle as before, with sides $5$ and $7.$ This time, what is the shortest possible length of the side $\\overline {BC},$ for which the figure is still a triangle?\n\nWe make the shortest possible segment $\\overline{BC}$ by decreasing the angle $\\angle{A}.$ Again, we fix $A$ and $B$, and then \"rotate\" the side $\\overline{AC}$ about the vertex $A$ towards the side $\\overline{AB}.$\n\n![](images/7d/7d1bd3363c2c218c6a2813a957bace3d29b7240919ced63d58be299e9478c099.png)\n\n         \n\nFrom the picture, we can see that for $m\\angle{A}=0^\\circ$ we will not have a triangle anymore and the length of $\\overline{BC}$ will be $7-5=2.$ Therefore, if we would like to have a triangle, $\\overline{BC}$ must be longer than the difference of the other sides: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & >AB-AC \\\\  & >7-5 \\\\  & >2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is true for all triangles: \n\nIn every triangle, the length of any one side must be larger than the difference of the other two sides.\n\nThe above statement is the other part of the **triangle inequality theorem**." 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186084511 1064 "All of the discussion so far can be summarized in the following statement that is called the **triangle inequality theorem**.\n\n*In a triangle, the length of a side must be greater than the difference of the lengths of the other two sides and less than the sum of those lengths.*\n\nLet's write that down using mathematical notations. Suppose that in a triangle the length of a side equals $a$ and the other two sides have length $b$ and $c$ with $b>c.$ Then, we must have \n\n$$\nb - c < a < b + c.\n$$\n\nIf we would like to omit the assumption that $b>c$, we can still use the same inequality, as long as we include an absolute value on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n| b - c |  < a < b + c\n$$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186084511 1064 "All of the discussion so far can be summarized in the following statement that is called the **triangle inequality theorem**.\n\n> *In a triangle, the length of a side must be greater than the difference of the lengths of the other two sides and less than the sum of those lengths.*\n\nLet's write that down using mathematical notations. Suppose that in a triangle the length of a side equals $a$ and the other two sides have length $b$ and $c$ with $b>c$. Then, we must have\n$b - c < a < b + c$.\n\nIf we would like to omit the assumption that $b>c$, we can still use the same inequality, as long as we include an absolute value on the left-hand side:\n$\\mid b - c \\mid < a < b + c$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186104680 1078 #edb/ref 17592186104684 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186105557 1071 "The diagram below shows a circle of radius $4$, a point $C$ that lies on the circle, and a third point $P$ on the plane.\n\n![](images/e6/e6ede881702a8713cf9c878f9a9d7fbdd0bdcb14bcab500d3cf256cc9cc84463.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true about the diagram above?\n\n1. $OP \\leq 4$\n2. $OC = 4$\n3. $OP > 4$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186105557 1071 "The diagram below shows a circle of radius $4,$ a point $C$ that lies on the circle, and a third point $P$ on the plane.\n\n \n\n![](images/e6/e6ede881702a8713cf9c878f9a9d7fbdd0bdcb14bcab500d3cf256cc9cc84463.png)\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true about the diagram above?\n\nI. $OP \\leq 4$\n\nII. $OC = 4$\n\nIII. $OP > 4$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186105557 1074 "The point $C$ lies on the circle itself. So, the distance from $O$ to $C$ is equal to the radius of the circle:\n\n$$\nOC = 4.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the point $P$ lies strictly outside the circle. So, the distance between $P$ and the center $O$ of the circle must be greater than the radius:\n\n$$\nOP > OC = 4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, statements II and III are true, while statement I is false." 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186105557 1074 "The point $C$ lies on the circle itself. So, the distance from $O$ to $C$ is equal to the radius of the circle:\n$OC = 4$.\n\nOn the other hand, the point $P$ lies strictly outside the circle. So, the distance between $P$ and the center $O$ of the circle must be greater than the radius:\n\n$$\nOP > OC = 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, statements II and III are true, while statement I is false." 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186105558 1071 "Consider the diagram below. What is the measure of $\\overline{AB}$ if $OQ=3$?\n\n![](images/4e/4e32346cc949b0f17ed219e44d4600abd23a0839a3855fcf76ff4060f322698c.png)" 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186105558 1071 "Consider the diagram below. What is the measure of $\\overline{AB}$ if $OQ=3?$\n\n![](images/4e/4e32346cc949b0f17ed219e44d4600abd23a0839a3855fcf76ff4060f322698c.png)" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186105558 1074 "Notice that the points $A$, $B$, and $Q$ lie on the circle. From the diagram, the radius of the circle is $r =OQ=3$.\n\nTo find $AB$, we first notice that\n\n$$\nAB = AO + OB\n$$\n\nNow, $\\overline{AO}$ and $\\overline{OB}$ are both segments between the center of the circle and the circle itself. So, they are both radii of the circle, and because the radius is $3$, we have\n\n$$\nAO=3, \\qquad OB=3\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nAB = AO+OB = 3 + 3= 6\n$$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186105558 1074 "Notice that the points $A,$ $B,$ and $Q$ lie on the circle. From the diagram, the radius of the circle is $r =OQ=3.$ \n\nTo find $AB,$ we first notice that\n\n$$\nAB = AO + OB.\n$$\n\nNow, $\\overline{AO}$ and $\\overline{OB}$ are both segments between the center of the circle and the circle itself. So, they are both radii of the circle, and because the radius is $3,$ we have\n\n$$\nAO=3, \\qquad OB=3.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nAB = AO+OB = 3 + 3= 6.\n$$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186105559 1071 "![](images/60/606c8bb2352215fe5c34acb78742034df016b8f41c7a6194b700e861f4f35596.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true about the diagram above?\n\n1. $\\overline{BC}$ is a chord of the circle\n2. $\\overline{BC}$ is a diameter of the circle\n3. $\\overline{OC}$ is a radius of the circle" 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186105559 1071 "![](images/60/606c8bb2352215fe5c34acb78742034df016b8f41c7a6194b700e861f4f35596.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true about the diagram above?\n\nI. $\\overline{BC}$ is a chord of the circle\n\nII. $\\overline{BC}$ is a diameter of the circle\n\nIII. $\\overline{OC}$ is a radius of the circle" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186105559 1074 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. As we can see, both endpoints $B$ and $C$ lie on the circle. Therefore,  $\\overline{BC}$ is a chord.\n\n- Statement II is true. As we can see, both endpoints $B$ and $C$ lie on the circle and $\\overline{BC}$ passes through the center $O.$ Therefore, $\\overline{BC}$ is a diameter.\n\n- Statement III is true. The segment $\\overline{OC}$ connects a point on the circle with its center. So, $\\overline{OC}$ is a radius.\n\nIn conclusion, statements I, II, and III are true." 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186105559 1074 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. As we can see, both endpoints $B$ and $C$ lie on the circle. Therefore, $\\overline{BC}$ is a chord.\n- Statement II is true. As we can see, both endpoints $B$ and $C$ lie on the circle and $\\overline{BC}$ passes through the center $O$. Therefore, $\\overline{BC}$ is a diameter.\n- Statement III is true. The segment $\\overline{OC}$ connects a point on the circle with its center. So, $\\overline{OC}$ is a radius.\n\nIn conclusion, statements I, II, and III are true." 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186105693 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497339 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186105693 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497367 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186105694 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497369 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186105694 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497412 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186105695 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497398 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186106022 1050 420.0 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186106422 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186106422 1071 "![](images/3f/3f772ad4b54217a479b63a6919a915af7bd8f44013687dae29712bf09624c68b.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true about the diagram above?\n\n1. $AB^(―)$ is a diameter of the circle\n2. $BC^(―)$ is a radius of the circle\n3. $OA^(―)$ is a chord of the circle" 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186106422 1071 "![](images/3f/3f772ad4b54217a479b63a6919a915af7bd8f44013687dae29712bf09624c68b.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true about the diagram above?\n\nI. $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of the circle\n\nII. $\\overset{―}{BC}$ is a radius of the circle\n\nIII. $\\overset{―}{OA}$ is a chord of the circle" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186106422 1074 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. As we can see, both endpoints $A$ and $B$ lie on the circle and $\\overset{―}{AB}$ passes through the center $O.$ Therefore, $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter.\n\n- Statement II is false since both endpoints of $B$ and $C$ lie on the circle. So, $\\overset{―}{BC}$ is a chord but not a radius.\n\n- Statement III is false since the endpoint $O$ does not lie on the circle. So, $\\overset{―}{OA}$ is a radius but not a chord.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true." 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186107061 1064 "Let $O$ be a point on the plane. Let's consider all the points on the plane whose distance to $O$ is equal to $5\\,\\textrm{cm}$.\n\n![](images/ba/ba0fc787b4469b0322fa46fa072d58ae163e24dea284f3e5507306e9b30dfbf3.png)\n\nThe collection of all such points defines a geometrical shape which is called a **circle**.\n\n- The point $O$ is called the **center** of the circle.\n- The distance from $O$ to any point on the circle is called the **radius**.\n\nIn our diagram above, the radius is\n\n$$\nr={\\color{black}5\\,\\textrm{cm}}\n$$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186107061 1064 "Let $O$ be a point on the plane. Let's consider all the points on the plane whose distance to $O$ is equal to $5\\,\\textrm{cm}.$\n\n![](images/ba/ba0fc787b4469b0322fa46fa072d58ae163e24dea284f3e5507306e9b30dfbf3.png)\n\nThe collection of all such points defines a geometrical shape which is called a **circle**. \n\n- The point $O$ is called the **center** of the circle.\n\n- The distance from $O$ to any point on the circle is called the **radius**.\n\nIn our diagram above, the radius is $r={\\color{black}5\\,\\textrm{cm}}.$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186107062 1064 "There are three important line segments associated with a circle:\n\n- A **radius** is any line segment that connects a point on the circle with the center (left-hand diagram).\n\n- A **chord** is any line segment whose endpoints lie on the circle (middle diagram).\n\n- A **diameter** is any chord that passes through the center of the circle (right-hand diagram). The diameter is the longest chord and its measure is twice the radius of the circle:\n\n$$\nd = 2r.\n$$\n\n![](images/7f/7f12a9db3210054e64397e26c87fd57ce4e55ff5dc9a2ed0d3b0ce3a8f9c2e65.png)" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186107062 1064 "There are three important line segments associated with a circle:\n\n- A **radius** is any line segment that connects a point on the circle with the center (left-hand diagram).\n- A **chord** is any line segment whose endpoints lie on the circle (middle diagram).\n- A **diameter** is any chord that passes through the center of the circle (right-hand diagram). The diameter is the longest chord and its measure is twice the radius of the circle:\n$d = 2r$.\n\n![](images/7f/7f12a9db3210054e64397e26c87fd57ce4e55ff5dc9a2ed0d3b0ce3a8f9c2e65.png)" 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186110619 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497364 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186110620 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497390 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186110761 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497333 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186119749 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497416 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186123740 1075 #edb/ref 17592186123741 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186123740 1075 #edb/ref 17592186123742 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186123740 1075 #edb/ref 17592186123743 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186123740 1075 #edb/ref 17592186123744 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186123740 1075 #edb/ref 17592186123745 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186123740 1075 #edb/ref 17592186496664 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186123740 1075 #edb/ref 17592186496700 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186123740 1075 #edb/ref 17592186496894 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186123740 1075 #edb/ref 17592186496982 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186123740 1075 #edb/ref 17592186497043 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186123740 1078 #edb/ref 17592186496982 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186124464 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497401 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186125190 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186125190 1071 "Consider the plane curve $xy = x^{2} + y^{2} - 7$. Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(2,-1)$." 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186125190 1071 "Consider the plane curve $xy={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-7.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(2,-1).$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186125190 1074 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-7 \\\\ 0 & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy-7$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,-1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,-1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,-1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy-7)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy-7)\\,j \\\\  & =(2x-y)\\,i+(2y-x)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-1) & =(2(2)-(-1))\\,i+(2(-1)-2)\\,j \\\\  & =5\\,i-4\\,j \\\\  & =⟨5,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(2,-1)=⟨5,-4⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(2,-1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{5} & =\\frac{y-(-1)}{-4} \\\\ -4(x-2) & =5(y+1) \\\\ -4x-5y+8-5 & =0 \\\\ -4x-5y+3 & =0 \\\\ 4x+5y-3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186147176 1071 "The number of hedgehogs $N(t)$ in a particular nature reserve after $t$ years is modeled by the function\n\n$$\nN(t) = 30 \\cdot 2^t.\n$$\n\nHow many years will it take for the population of hedgehogs to increase to $7\\,680?$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186147176 1071 "The number of hedgehogs $N(t)$ in a particular nature reserve after $t$ years is modeled by the function\n\n$N(t) = 30 \\cdot 2^t$.\n\nHow many years will it take for the population of hedgehogs to increase to $7\\,680$?" 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186147176 1074 "To find the time it takes for the number of hedgehogs to reach $7\\,680$, we need to solve the equation $N(t)=7\\,680$ for $t$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN(t) &= 7680 \\\\\n30 \\cdot 2^{t} &= 7680 \\\\\n2^{t} &= \\frac{7680}{30} \\\\\n2^{t} &= 256 \\\\\n2^{t} &= 2^{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it will take $t=8$ years for the population of hedgehogs to increase to $7\\,680$." 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186147176 1074 "To find the time it takes for the number of hedgehogs to reach $7\\,680,$ we need to solve the equation $N(t)=7\\,680$ for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =7\\,680 \\\\ 30\\cdot {2}^{t} & =7\\,680 \\\\ {2}^{t} & =\\frac{7\\,680}{30} \\\\ {2}^{t} & =256 \\\\ {2}^{t} & ={2}^{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it will take $t=8$ years for the population of hedgehogs to increase to $7\\,680.$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186147177 1071 "Bob invests $\\[math]250$ into some high-growth stocks, and the value of his investment doubles every year. How long will it take for Bob's investment to increase to $\\[/math]16\\,000$?" 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186147177 1071 "Bob invests $\\[math]250$ into some high-growth stocks, and the value of his investment doubles every year. How long will it take for Bob's investment to increase to $\\[/math]16\\,000?$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186147177 1074 "We know that Bob's investment doubles every year. So the value of Bob's investment, which we'll call $y(t)$, grows exponentially with a growth factor of $2$.\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth factor $b$) is\n\n$$\ny(t) = ab^t\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have the following:\n\n- $a=250$ is the initial investment (in dollars).\n- $b=2$ is the growth factor.\n- $t$ is the time, in years, since the investment was first made.\n\nTherefore, the expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t) = 250\\cdot 2^t\n$$\n\nNow, to find the time taken for the investment to reach $\\[math]16\\,000$, we need to solve the equation\n\n$$\ny(t) = 16\\,000\n$$\n\nfor $t$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(t) &= 16000 \\\\\n250 \\cdot 2^{t} &= 16000 \\\\\n2^{t} &= \\frac{16000}{250} \\\\\n2^{t} &= 64 \\\\\n2^{t} &= 2^{6} \\\\\nt &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it will take $6$ years for Bob's investment to grow to $\\[/math]16\\,000$." 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186147177 1074 "We know that Bob's investment doubles every year. So the value of Bob's investment, which we'll call $y(t),$ grows exponentially with a growth factor of $2.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth factor $b$) is\n\n$$\ny(t) = ab^t.\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have the following:\n\n- $a=250$ is the initial investment (in dollars).\n\n- $b=2$ is the growth factor.\n\n- $t$ is the time, in years, since the investment was first made.\n\nTherefore, the expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t) = 250\\cdot 2^t.\n$$\n\nNow, to find the time taken for the investment to reach $\\[math]16\\,000,$  we need to solve the equation $y(t) = 16\\,000$ for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =16\\,000 \\\\ 250\\cdot {2}^{t} & =16\\,000 \\\\ {2}^{t} & =\\frac{16\\,000}{250} \\\\ {2}^{t} & =64 \\\\ {2}^{t} & ={2}^{6} \\\\ t & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it will take $6$ years for Bob's investment to grow to $\\[/math]16\\,000.$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186147178 1071 "Mary is conducting a scientific experiment with bacteria. She notices that the number of bacteria in a Petri dish grows by $15\\%$ each day. If there were initially $100$ bacteria in the dish, what equation does Mary need to solve to predict how long it will take for the number of bacteria to increase to $535$?" 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186147178 1071 "Mary is conducting a scientific experiment with bacteria. She notices that the number of bacteria in a Petri dish grows by $15\\%$ each day. If there were initially $100$ bacteria in the dish, what equation does Mary need to solve to predict how long it will take for the number of bacteria to increase to $535?$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186147178 1074 "The number of bacteria $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $15\\%$.\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny = a(1+r)^t\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have the following:\n\n- $a=100$ is the initial number of bacteria.\n- $r = 0.15$ is the growth rate.\n- $t$ is the time, in days, since the experiment is started.\n\nSo, the expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t) = 100\\cdot(1+0.15)^t = 100\\cdot (1.15)^t\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find the time it will take for the number of bacteria to reach $535$, we would need to solve the equation $y(t)= 535$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n100 \\cdot (1.15)^{t} &= 535\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186147178 1074 "The number of bacteria $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $15\\%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny = a(1+r)^t.\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have the following:\n\n- $a=100$ is the initial number of bacteria.\n\n- $r = 0.15$ is the growth rate.\n\n- $t$ is the time, in days, since the experiment is started.\n\nSo, the expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t) = 100\\cdot(1+0.15)^t = 100\\cdot (1.15)^t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find the time it will take for the number of bacteria to reach $535,$ we would need to solve the equation $y(t)= 535,$ which gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100\\cdot (1.15)^{t} & =535.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186147179 1071 "Some scientists are monitoring the growth of the wolf population in a particular nature reserve. The population of wolves $W(t)$ after $t$ decades of observations is given by\n\n$$\nW(t)  = W_{0} \\cdot 4^{t},\n$$\n\nwhere $W_{0}$ represents the wolves' population at the beginning of the study. If there were $3\\,200$ wolves after $3$ decades, how many wolves were there at the beginning of the study?" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186147179 1071 "Some scientists are monitoring the growth of the wolf population in a particular nature reserve. The population of wolves $W(t)$ after $t$ decades of observations is given by\n\n$W(t) = W_{0} \\cdot 4^{t}$,\n\nwhere $W_{0}$ represents the wolves' population at the beginning of the study. If there were $3\\,200$ wolves after $3$ decades, how many wolves were there at the beginning of the study?" 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186147179 1074 "There are $3\\,200$  wolves  $3$ decades after the scientists started studying the wolf population, so $W(3)  = 3\\,200.$\n\nTo find the initial number of wolves $W_0,$ we substitute $t=3$ and $W(3)  = 3\\,200$ into the given equation and solve for $W_0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(3) & ={W}_{0}\\cdot {4}^{3} \\\\ 3\\,200 & ={W}_{0}\\cdot 64 \\\\ {W}_{0} & =\\frac{3\\,200}{64} \\\\ {W}_{0} & =50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there were $50$ wolves at the beginning of the study." 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186147179 1074 "There are $3\\,200$ wolves $3$ decades after the scientists started studying the wolf population, so\n\n$$\nW(3) = 3\\,200\n$$\n\nTo find the initial number of wolves $W_0$, we substitute $t=3$ and\n\n$$\nW(3) = 3\\,200\n$$\n\ninto the given equation and solve for $W_0$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW(3) &= W_{0} \\cdot 4^{3} \\\\\n3200 &= W_{0} \\cdot 64 \\\\\nW_{0} &= \\frac{3200}{64} \\\\\nW_{0} &= 50\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there were $50$ wolves at the beginning of the study." 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186147421 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497346 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186147421 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497357 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186147422 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497370 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186147422 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497384 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186147423 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497341 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186147423 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497363 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186147424 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497330 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186147424 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497382 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186147664 1050 540.0 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186148795 1064 "We can use our knowledge of solving exponential equations to obtain information about a system that follows an exponential growth model.\n\nFor example, suppose that the revenue $R(t)$, in millions of dollars, of a rapidly growing startup company is given by the function\n\n$$\nR(t) = 2\\cdot 3^t\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the number of years since the company was incorporated. How long will it take for the company's revenue to reach $\\[math]18$ million?\n\nTo find the solution to this problem, we need to solve the equation $R(t) = 18$. This gives us the equation\n\n$$\n2\\cdot 3^t = 18\n$$\n\nTo solve this equation, we first divide both sides by $2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 \\cdot 3^{t} &= 18 \\\\\n\\frac{2 \\cdot 3^{t}}{2} &= \\frac{18}{2} \\\\\n\\frac{2 \\cdot 3^{t}}{2} &= 9 \\\\\n3^{t} &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we notice that $9$ is a power of $3$, because $9=3^2$. So we get\n\n$$\n3^{\\color{blue}t} = 3^{\\color{blue}2}\n$$\n\nThe bases of both exponents are equal, so the powers must also be equal. Therefore, our solution is\n\n$$\nt = {\\color{blue}2}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that it will take the company $\\color{blue}2$ years to reach a revenue of $\\[/math]18$ million." 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186148795 1064 "We can use our knowledge of solving exponential equations to obtain information about a system that follows an exponential growth model.\n\nFor example, suppose that the revenue $R(t),$ in millions of dollars, of a rapidly growing startup company is given by the function\n\n$$\nR(t) = 2\\cdot 3^t,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the number of years since the company was incorporated. How long will it take for the company's revenue to reach $\\[math]18$ million?\n\nTo find the solution to this problem, we need to solve the equation $R(t) = 18.$ This gives us the equation\n\n$$\n2\\cdot 3^t = 18.\n$$\n\nTo solve this equation, we first divide both sides by $2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot {3}^{t} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot {3}^{t}}{2} & =\\frac{18}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}\\cdot {3}^{t}}{\\cancel{2}} & =9 \\\\ {3}^{t} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we notice that $9$ is a power of $3,$ because $9=3^2.$ So we get\n\n$$\n3^{\\color{blue}t} = 3^{\\color{blue}2}.\n$$\n\nThe bases of both exponents are equal, so the powers must also be equal. Therefore, our solution is\n\n$$\nt = {\\color{blue}2}.\n$$\n\nWe conclude that it will take the company $\\color{blue}2$ years to reach a revenue of $\\[/math]18$ million." 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186148796 1064 "We often need to know the system's state at $t=0$ when using an exponential growth model.\n\nFor example, suppose that the number of users $N(t)$ of a new phone app can be modeled by the function\n\n$$\nN(t) = N_0(1.2)^t\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ represents the time, in weeks, since the app launched, and $N_0$ is a constant. If there are $5\\,000$ users $5$ weeks after launch, can we determine how many users the app had when it launched?\n\nHere, $N_0$ represents the initial number of users, and this is what we're trying to find.\n\nFrom the given information, we know that\n\n$$\nN(5) = 5\\,000\n$$\n\nSo let's plug this into our function:\n\n$$\n5\\,000 = N_0(1.2)^5\n$$\n\nTo solve this equation, we can divide both sides by $(1.2)^5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(5000)/((1.2)^{5}) &= (N_{0}(1.2)^{5})/((1.2)^{5}) \\\\\n(5000)/((1.2)^{5}) &= (N_{0}(1.2)^{5})/((1.2)^{5}) \\\\\n(5000)/((1.2)^{5}) &= N_{0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's flip the equation around to make it easier to work with.\n\n$$\nN_0 = \\dfrac{5\\,000}{(1.2)^5}\n$$\n\nWe can now work out $N_0$ using a calculator. We find that\n\n$$\nN_0\\approx 2\\,009\n$$\n\nWe have rounded to the nearest integer since $N_0$ refers to a number of people.\n\nTherefore, when it launched, the app had $2\\,009$ users." 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186148796 1064 "We often need to know the system's state at $t=0$ when using an exponential growth model.\n\nFor example, suppose that the number of users $N(t)$ of a new phone app can be modeled by the function\n\n$$\nN(t) = N_0(1.2)^t,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ represents the time, in weeks, since the app launched, and $N_0$ is a constant. If there are $5\\,000$ users $5$ weeks after launch, can we determine how many users the app had when it launched?\n\nHere, $N_0$ represents the initial number of users, and this is what we're trying to find.\n\nFrom the given information, we know that $N(5) = 5\\,000,$ So let's plug this into our function:\n\n$$\n5\\,000 = N_0(1.2)^5\n$$\n\nTo solve this equation, we can divide both sides by $(1.2)^5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5\\,000}{(1.2)^{5}} & =\\frac{{N}_{0}(1.2)^{5}}{(1.2)^{5}} \\\\ \\frac{5\\,000}{(1.2)^{5}} & =\\frac{{N}_{0}\\cancel{(1.2)^{5}}}{\\cancel{(1.2)^{5}}} \\\\ \\frac{5\\,000}{(1.2)^{5}} & ={N}_{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's flip the equation around to make it easier to work with. \n\n$$\nN_0 = \\dfrac{5\\,000}{(1.2)^5}\n$$\n\nWe can now work out $N_0$ using a calculator. We find that\n\n$$\nN_0\\approx 2\\,009.\n$$\n\nWe have rounded to the nearest integer since $N_0$ refers to a number of people.\n\nTherefore, when it launched, the app had $2\\,009$ users." 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186149614 1075 #edb/ref 17592186496956 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186149614 1078 #edb/ref 17592186496956 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186149616 1075 #edb/ref 17592186496846 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186149616 1078 #edb/ref 17592186496846 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186150521 1078 #edb/ref 17592186150524 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186151827 1071 "| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $6$ | $4$ |\n| $9$ | $6$ |\n| $15$ | $10$ |\n\nThe table above shows a proportional relationship. Assuming that $y=kx$ for each pair of values, what is the constant of proportionality?" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186151827 1071 "| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $6$ | $4$ |\n| $9$ | $6$ |\n| $15$ | $10$ |\n\nThe table above shows a proportional relationship. What is the constant of proportionality?" 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186151827 1074 "We're told that $x$ and $y$ have a proportional relationship.\n\nTo find the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(x,y)$ from the table. Let's choose the point $(6,4)$, which corresponds to the first row of the table.\n\nNow, we can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-coordinate to the $x$-coordinate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nk &= \\frac{y}{x} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{6} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 \\div 2}{6 \\div 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the constant of proportionality is\n\n$$\nk=\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186151827 1074 "We're told that $x$ and $y$ have a proportional relationship.\n\nTo find the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(x,y)$ from the table. Let's choose the point $(6,4),$ which corresponds to the first row of the table.\n\nNow, we can