y ).$ The Wronskian satisfies $W(2)=0.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies the following first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)={{field-1}}W(x)\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we get\n\n$$\nW={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNow, using the fact that $W(2)=0,$ we obtain \n{{field-3}}." 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186217801 1071 "$y^{″} + \\frac{1}{x}y^{′} + Q(x)y = 0, x > 0$\nLet $y_{1}$ and $y_{2}$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(0, ∞)$. The Wronskian satisfies $W(2) = 0$. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies the following first-order differential equation:\n$-\\frac{1}{x}$ {{field-1}}" 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186217801 1074 "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{1}{x}W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\frac{1}{x}W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{x}^{-1} \\\\ W & =\\frac{C}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(2)=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =\\frac{C}{2} \\\\ 0 & =\\frac{C}{2} \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)=0\\text{ for all }x>0.$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186218358 1064 "The Wronskian is a quick way to check whether two solutions of a homogeneous ODE are linearly independent.\n\nIn this lesson, we learn an identity, which tells us how the Wronskian changes with $x$ for an equation of the form \n\n$$\ny''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nSuppose $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of the second-order linear homogeneous ODE above on some interval $I.$ Let $W(x)$ be their Wronskian:\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & {y}_{2}(x) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{'}(x) & {y}_{2}^{'}(x)\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nThen, **Abel's identity** states that the Wronskian satisfies the first-order linear differential equation\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nSolving this equation via separation of variables gives the general form for $W(x){:}$\n\n$$\nW(x) = C e^{-\\int P(x) \\, \\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nThis allows us to determine the behavior of the Wronskian without knowing the solutions $y_1$ and $y_2.$\n\nFor example, consider the second-order homogeneous differential equation \n\n$$\ny''+3xy'+e^x y=0\n$$\n\nwith solutions $y_1$ and $y_2,$ and the corresponding Wronskian $W(x).$ Let's determine the differential equation that $W(x)$ must satisfy.\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of $y'$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=3x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into Abel's identity gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{'}(x) & =-3xW(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** If the equation is not already in standard form, we should rewrite it before applying Abel’s identity." 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186218358 1064 "The Wronskian is a quick way to check whether two solutions of a homogeneous ODE are linearly independent.\n\nIn this lesson, we learn an identity, which tells us how the Wronskian changes with $x$ for an equation of the form \n\n$$\ny''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nSuppose $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of the second-order linear homogeneous ODE above on some interval $I.$ Let $W(x)$ be their Wronskian:\n![](images/bf/bf982a342681e4142c476f52c77cb95836bc024d75a0c62961fc5f0d6d71320b.svg)\n\nThen, **Abel's identity** states that the Wronskian satisfies the first-order linear differential equation\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nSolving this equation via separation of variables gives the general form for $W(x){:}$\n\n$$\nW(x) = C e^{-\\int P(x) \\, \\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nThis allows us to determine the behavior of the Wronskian without knowing the solutions $y_1$ and $y_2.$\n\nFor example, consider the second-order homogeneous differential equation \n\n$$\ny''+3xy'+e^x y=0\n$$\n\nwith solutions $y_1$ and $y_2,$ and the corresponding Wronskian $W(x).$ Let's determine the differential equation that $W(x)$ must satisfy.\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of $y'$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=3x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into Abel's identity gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′}(x) & =-3xW(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** If the equation is not already in standard form, we should rewrite it before applying Abel’s identity." 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186218359 1064 "*Abel's identity* says that if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of\n\n$$\ny''+p(x)y'+q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their *Wronskian* $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-p(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nTo prove it, we start with the definition\n\n$$\nW(x)=y_1y_2'-y_2y_1'\n$$\n\nand differentiate both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{'}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{'}) \\\\  & =\\cancel{{y}_{1}^{'}{y}_{2}^{'}}+{y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{''}-\\cancel{{y}_{2}^{'}{y}_{1}^{'}}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{''} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{''}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{''}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of $y''+p(x)y'+q(x)y=0$, we have\n\n$$\ny_1''=-p(x)y_1'-q(x)y_1\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\ny_2''=-p(x)y_2'-q(x)y_2.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute these into the expression for $W'(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{'}(x) & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{''}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{''} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(-p(x){y}_{2}^{'}-q(x){y}_{2})-{y}_{2}(-p(x){y}_{1}^{'}-q(x){y}_{1}) \\\\  & =-p(x){y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{'}-\\cancel{q(x){y}_{1}{y}_{2}}+p(x){y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{'}+\\cancel{q(x){y}_{1}{y}_{2}} \\\\  & =-p(x)({y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{'}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{'}) \\\\  & =-p(x)W(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $W'(x)=-p(x)W(x)$, as required." 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186218359 1064 "*Abel's identity* says that if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of\n\n$$\ny''+p(x)y'+q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their *Wronskian* $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-p(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nTo prove it, we start with the definition\n\n$$\nW(x)=y_1y_2'-y_2y_1'\n$$\n\nand differentiate both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}) \\\\  & =\\cancel{{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}}+{y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{″}-\\cancel{{y}_{2}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{″} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{″}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of $y''+p(x)y'+q(x)y=0$, we have\n\n$$\ny_1''=-p(x)y_1'-q(x)y_1\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\ny_2''=-p(x)y_2'-q(x)y_2.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute these into the expression for $W'(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′}(x) & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{″}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{″} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(-p(x){y}_{2}^{′}-q(x){y}_{2})-{y}_{2}(-p(x){y}_{1}^{′}-q(x){y}_{1}) \\\\  & =-p(x){y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-\\cancel{q(x){y}_{1}{y}_{2}}+p(x){y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}+\\cancel{q(x){y}_{1}{y}_{2}} \\\\  & =-p(x)({y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}) \\\\  & =-p(x)W(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $W'(x)=-p(x)W(x)$, as required." 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186228100 1078 #edb/ref 17592186228104 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186229160 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497342 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186229161 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497371 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186229191 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497353 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186229191 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497372 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186229646 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186229646 1071 "For which of the following values is the Runge-Kutta Method (RK4) stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\nI. $\\lam