1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1)| & =|1-1+\\frac{(1)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(1)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(1)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{3}{8}| \\\\  & =0.375<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2)| & =|1-2+\\frac{(2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(2)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(2)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{3}| \\\\  & =0.333…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(3)| & =|1-3+\\frac{(3)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(3)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(3)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{11}{8}| \\\\  & =1.375<1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the RK4 Method is unstable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only\".\n\nNote that the stability region of the RK4 Method is $0<\\lambda Δx<2.785…,$ so it is not absolutely stable." 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186231405 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497356 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186231406 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497355 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186231406 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497410 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186243954 1071 "Consider the following polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=2+{t}^{2},\\,{p}_{2}(t)=1-t+2{t}^{2},\\,q(t)=6+2t.\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\},$ and $[q(t)]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186243954 1071 "Consider the following polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=2+{t}^{2},\\,{p}_{2}(t)=1-t+2{t}^{2},\\,q(t)=6+2t.\n$$\n\nIf $B={{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)},$ and $[q(t)]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186243954 1074 "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)=q(t)\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(2+{t}^{2})+{x}_{2}(1-t+2{t}^{2}) & =6+2t \\\\ (2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2})-{x}_{2}t+({x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}){t}^{2} & =6+2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $t$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=6 \\\\ -{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=6 \\\\ {x}_{2}=-2 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations we get ${x}_{2}=-2.$  Substituting this into the first equation yields\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}+(-2)=6\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[q(t)]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=4+(-2)=2.$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186243954 1074 "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)=q(t)\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(2+{t}^{2})+{x}_{2}(1-t+2{t}^{2}) & =6+2t \\\\ (2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2})-{x}_{2}t+({x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}){t}^{2} & =6+2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $t$, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=6 \\\\ -{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=6 \\\\ {x}_{2}=-2 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations we get ${x}_{2}=-2.$  Substituting this into the first equation yields\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}+(-2)=6\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[q(t)]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=4+(-2)=2.$" 13194139535332 17592186045417 false] [17592186281684 1074 "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function. The region $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 3x\\}.\n$$\n\nHence, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\